  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1151 PM CDT Sat May 30 2026\n\nTypical summer pattern to start the week off as upper level ridge\naxis transitions across the Southern Plains for Monday and\nTuesday, bringing hot and dry conditions to the area. Main \nthermal axis seems to be across the Red River Valley and adjacent\nparts of Oklahoma and Texas, with highs Monday and Tuesday across\nthe northern Big Country near the 100 degree mark in some areas.\nIt will be a few degrees cooler farther south but still going to\nbe above normal.\n\nRidge is short lived however and shifts east as a weak upper\ntrough settles into northwest Mexico. This places much of West\nTexas into south to southwest flow aloft, with surface flow from\nthe Texas coast into the area. Precipitable water values will\nclimb as the moisture fetch continues. There will likely be a\nseries of weak shortwaves moving across New Mexico and West Texas\nfor Wednesday into the weekend, although the exact timing remains\nvery uncertain. Air mass will be weakly capped at best so showers\nand storms will likely be fairly widespread across West Texas\nand Eastern New Mexico, at least at times as the shortwaves move\nacross. How much of this can shift east into West Central Texas is\na question, with the highest POPs across the western half of the\narea instead of the usual eastern half. Given the uncertainty, not\nreally going to argue with the model blends for rain chances, but\nwill emphasize the fact that as we get closer to the event, the\nrain chances that are currently spread across multiple time\nperiods will likely consolidate into a few most likely periods.",
